**Already fixed today** (commits `1a0687d1`, `b2b736e1`): `orchestrator.functions.ts` PRD→Linear dispatch and `capabilities.functions.ts` (34-agent capability load) were both parallelized/batched.

**Still open — `src/lib/ai/eval-runner.server.ts:157-227`**, `runEvalSuite` does a single-row `insert` into `eval_case_results` inside the `for (const c of cases)` loop, at three separate call sites (error path, success path, catch path). For a suite with N cases this is N sequential round-trips purely for bookkeeping — the model calls above them are a real dependency and must stay sequential, but the writes don't need to.

```ts
// Before: insert() at 3 call sites inside the loop
async function runEvalSuite(...) {
  const scores: number[] = [];
  for (const c of cases) {
    try {
      const subject = await callModel(...);
      if (subject.status !== "ok") {
        await supabase.from("eval_case_results").insert({ ...row... });
        continue;
      }
      const judge = await callModel(...);
      await supabase.from("eval_case_results").insert({ ...row... });
    } catch (e) {
      await supabase.from("eval_case_results").insert({ ...row... });
    }
  }
}

// After: accumulate, batch-insert once
async function runEvalSuite(...) {
  const scores: number[] = [];
  const rows: EvalCaseResultInsert[] = [];
  for (const c of cases) {
    try {
      const subject = await callModel(...);
      if (subject.status !== "ok") {
        rows.push({ run_id: runId, case_id: c.id, user_id: userId, status: "error", ... });
        continue;
      }
      const judge = await callModel(...);
      rows.push({ run_id: runId, case_id: c.id, user_id: userId, status: isPassed ? "passed" : "failed", ... });
    } catch (e) {
      rows.push({ run_id: runId, case_id: c.id, user_id: userId, status: "error", error: ... });
    }
  }
  if (rows.length) await supabase.from("eval_case_results").insert(rows);
}
```

**Still open — `src/lib/lineage.functions.ts:353-362`**, `promotePrdToTasks` calls `recordLineage` (an `upsert`) once per created task in a loop:

```ts
for (const t of tasks ?? []) {
  await recordLineage(supabase, userId, {
    parent_kind: "prd", parent_id: prd.id, child_kind: "task", child_id: t.id,
    rationale: "Generated from PRD by promotePrdToTasks", created_by_agent: "prd-writer",
  });
}
```

`recordLineage` itself (`lineage.functions.ts:41-54`) is a thin wrapper around a single-row `upsert` with `onConflict: "user_id,parent_kind,parent_id,child_kind,child_id,relation"`. Since the conflict target is stable across rows, this batches cleanly:

```ts
const lineageRows = (tasks ?? []).map((t) => ({
  user_id: userId,
  parent_kind: "prd" as const,
  parent_id: prd.id,
  child_kind: "task" as const,
  child_id: t.id,
  relation: "promoted",
  rationale: "Generated from PRD by promotePrdToTasks",
  created_by_agent: "prd-writer",
}));
if (lineageRows.length) {
  await supabase
    .from("artifact_lineage")
    .upsert(lineageRows, { onConflict: "user_id,parent_kind,parent_id,child_kind,child_id,relation" });
}
```

Note the behavior change: today's per-row `recordLineage` call fails independently per row (nothing catches it, so actually the *first* failure currently aborts the loop — worth confirming that's intended); a single batch `upsert` fails or succeeds as one unit. Since this runs strictly after the real `tasks` insert already succeeded, a lineage-stamping failure here is not user-facing data loss — logging the error rather than throwing is reasonable if you want the batch to be forgiving.

**Still open — `src/components/knowledge/CalendarPanel.tsx:316-360`.** Four derived values (`allItems`, `feed`, `pastCount`, `buckets`) are computed as plain `const`s in the render body — filter/map/sort/reduce chains re-run on *every* render, including the ones triggered by the component's own `useMutation`'s `isPending` flips (line ~309, the "add hold" mutation).

```tsx
// Before — recomputed every render
const allItems = [...meetingItems, ...eventItems];
const _now = Date.now();
const _end = _now + 14 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000;
const feed = allItems.filter(...).sort(...);
const pastCount = allItems.filter(...).length;
const buckets: Record<string, DayItem[]> = {};
for (const it of allItems) { ... }
```

```tsx
// After
const allItems = useMemo(() => [...meetingItems, ...eventItems], [meetingItems, eventItems]);

const feed = useMemo(() => {
  const now = Date.now();
  const end = now + 14 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000;
  return allItems
    .filter((it) => {
      const t = new Date(it.start_at).getTime();
      return t >= now - 12 * 60 * 60 * 1000 && t <= end;
    })
    .sort((a, b) => new Date(a.start_at).getTime() - new Date(b.start_at).getTime());
}, [allItems]);

const pastCount = useMemo(
  () => allItems.filter((it) => new Date(it.start_at).getTime() < Date.now() - 12 * 60 * 60 * 1000).length,
  [allItems],
);

const buckets = useMemo(() => {
  const b: Record<string, DayItem[]> = {};
  for (const it of allItems) {
    const k = new Date(it.start_at).toDateString();
    (b[k] = b[k] ?? []).push(it);
  }
  return b;
}, [allItems]);
```

★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────
Memoizing on `[allItems]` pins the "now" used for the 14-day window to the last time the *data* changed, not wall-clock time on every render — that's a real behavior change, not just a mechanical wrap. It's almost certainly the better behavior here (an item doesn't silently drop out of "upcoming" just because a re-render happened to fire a millisecond past midnight), but flag it because it's the kind of memoization that changes what the UI shows, not just how fast it renders.
─────────────────────────────────────────────────

**Already fixed today**: two memo-defeat suspects (`ThemeRow`, `SignalCard`) turned out to be false positives on inspection — parent already passes a stable top-level `useCallback` handler, no inline-arrow rewrap. `OpportunityQueue`'s `BoundOpportunityRow` wrapper (stable id/handler as props, per-row currying inside its own render rather than inside `.map()`) is the correct fix for "hooks can't live inside a loop, but a naive inline closure defeats `memo()`" — worth using as the template if you hit this pattern elsewhere.

